{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00719 - MONDO_5492",
  "verbalization": "Azatadine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Urticaria (disease) through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve the indication of Familial Dermatographia. The drug's association with Familial Dermatographia, in turn, is linked to other compounds that are known to induce Urticaria. This suggests a potential mechanism where Azatadine may influence the expression of genes or pathways associated with dermatographic reactions, leading to the development of Urticaria. The evidence is supported by the shared indication of Familial Dermatographia across multiple pathways, indicating a possible common biological mechanism or a convergent effect. The presence of multiple compounds—Phenyltoloxamine, Tripelennamine, and Antazoline—each linked to Urticaria further supports the idea that Azatadine may act through a broader network of pharmacological interactions that contribute to the manifestation of Urticaria. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering the broader pharmacological context in assessing the potential for drug reutilization.",
